From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Craig Johnson <1545411+captainpacket@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 4 Sep 2026 21:40:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] catalystwan: read the generated CSR back from the device record

devices_certificates reported "no CSR" for a CSR that exists. vManage generates
the CSR ON THE DEVICE and stores it on the DEVICE RECORD; the generate call's
own response carries inventory columns and no certificate. So read it back and
publish it under the same response key, in the legacy shape, on every version.

THIS REPLACES THE PREVIOUS 0003, WHICH ALSO DISPATCHED ON THE MANAGER VERSION.
That half was wrong and is deleted. The reasoning behind it was that the SDK
splits one endpoint in two:

    @versions("<20.16")   @post("/certificate/generate/csr", "data")
    def generate_csr(...)      -> DataSequence[DeviceCsrGenerationResponse]

    @versions(">=20.16")  @post("/certificate/generate/csr")
    def generate_csr_task(...) -> CertActionResponse   # id: UUID

which reads as "20.16 moved this endpoint to an async task". It did not, or at
least 20.18.4 did not. MEASURED, on a live box:

    TASK [Ask the device to generate a CSR (vmanage)]
    1 validation error for CertActionResponse
    id
      Field required [type=missing,
       input_value={'header': {'columns': [{...host-name': 'vmanage'}]}]

That is the LEGACY GRID -- header + data -- which is exactly what the pre-20.16
signature unwraps. Routing a 20.18 Manager to generate_csr_task made the SDK
parse a grid as a task object and die on the missing id, converting a
recoverable empty result into a hard failure in the middle of enrolment, after
the certificate-signing switch had already succeeded. The @versions annotations
describe the SDK's model of the appliance, not the appliance.

This is the SECOND patch in this fork built on an SDK-declared version rule
instead of a measurement; the first was patches/catalystwan/0001, deleted today
for the same reason. The rule is now: a version gate is a claim about the
appliance and needs an appliance to back it.

Controlled offline against catalystwan 0.41.5.dev2, feeding the observed
envelope to both models:

    NEG: CertActionResponse rejects the grid: ('id',) missing
    NEG: reproduces the measured failure exactly (id / missing)
    POS: generate_csr path parses 1 row(s) ->
         [{"deviceIP": "10.0.0.1", "host-name": "vmanage"}]
    POS: no deviceCSR in the grid row -> the device-record read-back is load-bearing

The last line is why only the dispatch half is dropped: the grid genuinely does
not carry the certificate, so the read-back is still the fix for the original
"no CSR" report, and netlab 0036 stays retired.

Both spellings are emitted (deviceCSR alias, device_csr field) because callers
in the wild read either. The read never overwrites a CSR the generate call
already returned, and a failed read cannot mask a generate that succeeded.

UPSTREAM ASK: FILE. The read-back is a plain bug fix against
cisco-en-programmability/ansible-collection-catalystwan.

---
diff --git a/plugins/modules/devices_certificates.py b/plugins/modules/devices_certificates.py
--- a/plugins/modules/devices_certificates.py
+++ b/plugins/modules/devices_certificates.py
@@ -213,6 +213,32 @@
             module.exit_json(**result.model_dump(mode="json"))
 
         payload = TargetDevice(deviceIP=target_device_details.device_ip)
+
+        # SKYFORGE FIX: READ THE CSR BACK FROM THE DEVICE RECORD.
+        #
+        # The generate call itself is left ALONE, deliberately. The SDK splits
+        # this one endpoint in two, by version:
+        #
+        #   @versions("<20.16")  @post("/certificate/generate/csr", "data")
+        #   def generate_csr(...)      -> DataSequence[DeviceCsrGenerationResponse]
+        #
+        #   @versions(">=20.16") @post("/certificate/generate/csr")
+        #   def generate_csr_task(...) -> CertActionResponse   # id: UUID
+        #
+        # and it is tempting to read that as "dispatch on the Manager version".
+        # WE TRIED THAT AND IT WAS WRONG. Measured against live vManage 20.18.4,
+        # the endpoint returns the LEGACY GRID -- {"header": {...}, "data": [...]}
+        # -- which is precisely what the pre-20.16 signature unwraps. Routing a
+        # 20.18 Manager to generate_csr_task made the SDK parse that grid as a
+        # task object and it died on the missing id, turning a recoverable empty
+        # result into a hard failure mid-enrolment. The @versions annotations
+        # describe the SDK's model of the appliance, not the appliance.
+        #
+        # What IS real is that the grid row carries no CSR: the columns are
+        # inventory fields (host-name, deviceIP, ...). vManage generates the CSR
+        # ON THE DEVICE and stores it on the DEVICE RECORD, so read it from
+        # there and publish it under the same response key. That is the whole
+        # defect -- the module reported "no CSR" for a CSR that exists.
         module.send_request_safely(
             result=result,
             action_name="Generate CSR for vManage",
@@ -221,6 +247,33 @@
             response_key="generate_csr",
         )
 
+        # RESTORE THE LEGACY RESPONSE SHAPE so a caller reading
+        # response.generate_csr[0].deviceCSR works on every Manager version and
+        # never needs to know which endpoint ran.
+        #
+        # Both spellings are emitted: the pydantic alias is deviceCSR, the model
+        # field is device_csr. Callers in the wild read either, and emitting one
+        # would silently break the other. The read never overwrites a CSR the
+        # generate call already returned, and a failed read cannot mask a
+        # generate that already succeeded.
+        try:
+            regenerated = get_target_device(module=module, device_category="controllers")
+            device_csr = getattr(regenerated, "device_csr", None)
+            if device_csr:
+                existing = result.response.get("generate_csr")
+                already_has_csr = (
+                    isinstance(existing, list)
+                    and existing
+                    and isinstance(existing[0], dict)
+                    and (existing[0].get("deviceCSR") or existing[0].get("device_csr"))
+                )
+                if not already_has_csr:
+                    result.response["generate_csr"] = [
+                        {"deviceCSR": device_csr, "device_csr": device_csr}
+                    ]
+        except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001 - the generate call already succeeded; this is enrichment
+            pass
+
     if module.params.get("send_to_controllers"):
         module.execute_action_safely(
             result,
-- 
